WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.390
This video is sponsored by Notion.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:07.600
While most of us access the internet through
a wireless device, a physical link is still

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.600
required.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.670
My website is hosted on a server in North
America.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.849
If you’re viewing this on another continent,
your internet provider has almost certainly

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:18.210
used a cable under the sea to retrieve the
data.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:22.860
Lines just a tad thicker than a garden hose
crisscross the ocean, zipping data from LA

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.330
to Chile faster than the time it takes to
finish this sentence.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.680
These things are absolute marvels of engineering.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.120
This isn’t your grandparents’ twisted
pair of copper wire from the telephone pole

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.120
to the house.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:40.890
There are more than 400 lines in service stretching
over 1.3 million km or 800,000 miles.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.879
Enough to go around the world 32 times.

00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:48.559
Data travels as pulses of light inside the
cables' optical fibers before arriving at

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:52.339
a landing station where it continues its journey
overland.

00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:55.570
Laying cables isn’t new and dates back to the 1800s.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.859
The first transatlantic telegraph cable allowed
Queen Victoria to send a message to U.S.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:05.080
President James Buchanan in 1858, opening
up a new era in global communication.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.350
During the Cold War, an American nuclear submarine
secretly listened in when the Soviets used

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.800
an underwater cable to communicate between
a naval base and its mainland.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.540
Today, modern cables carry digital data, including
internet traffic.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.280
And many of these have recently been funded
by tech companies which now own or lease

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.501
well over half of the availability bandwidth.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:30.600
Facebook and Google are working with regional
telecommunications providers to invest in

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.140
a new line called Echo to connect the US with
Asia.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.420
The tech giants may share the cable, but they’ll
have their own designated fiber pairs within

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.420
it.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.560
Kind of like having your own lane on a multi-lane
highway.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.810
So, why are tech companies laying their own
cables?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.480
They used to lease bandwidth from telecom
providers like AT&amp;T who were the major investors

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:50.680
in building cables.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.270
But now, they’re opting to build their own
pipes to meet the surge in demand for their

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.330
services - from YouTube, to Spotify, to cloud
computing.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:02.220
By building lines themselves, they have greater
control over the infrastructure.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.340
Hurricane Sandy was the wake-up call.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.619
The superstorm that hit the US east coast
in 2012 damaged an area that hosts a significant

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:13.989
number of cable landing points, disrupting
communication for days.

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:17.959
Facebook and Microsoft realized it wasn’t
the best idea to have the cables clustered

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:19.730
in New York and New Jersey.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.829
So, they partnered with telecommunications
infrastructure company Telxius to design a

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:28.549
line further south - opting to make Virginia
Beach the starting point for a new connection

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:29.640
to Spain.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.129
When Marea went operational in 2019, it provided
the fastest transatlantic internet connection

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:44.336
ever - transferring 160 terabits of data every
second - equivalent to streaming 71 million HD movies at the same time.

00:02:44.336 --> 00:02:50.091
The capacity on these cables is just enormous, it’s nothing like we’ve seen three, four years ago.

00:02:50.091 --> 00:02:54.415
Used to be you’d build a cable and there might be four fiber pairs on the cable. 

00:02:54.415 --> 00:02:58.500
Now it’s routine for these new cables to come online with like 16 fiber pairs.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.970
Tech giants are also choosing to fund the
project entirely themselves.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.969
Google announced in June it’s building a
private cable called Firmina running from

00:03:05.969 --> 00:03:10.109
the U.S. to South America, which it says will
be the longest line in the world.

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:15.060
By building privately, Google can decide exactly
where to build routes to best suit its needs.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.889
For example, it can minimize the distance
between its data centers which then reduces

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:20.390
delay known as latency.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.810
It can connect remote parts of the world.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.700
Or reinforce an area with limited lines.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.859
Google has built six cables on its own dime
and has funded 16 projects overall.

00:03:30.859 --> 00:03:34.770
Tech giants that already wield significant
influence are likely to own more of the internet

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.340
backbone in the future.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.400
Kind of like if Amazon owned the roads where
packages are delivered.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.919
I think the concern is that the big content
providers would form some sort of monopoly

00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:48.340
on international bandwidth but they're not sellers of bandwidth, right? 

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.500
Like, they deploy it and use it themselves.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.849
These companies fork over hundreds of millions
of dollars to build the infrastructure which

00:03:55.849 --> 00:03:57.269
can take years.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:00.530
You won’t see cables at the beach because
they’re buried below the seabed.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:05.819
In the deep sea, large ships lay them on the
ocean floor, sometimes 8,000 meters or 26,000

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:09.489
ft below - as deep as Mount Everest is high.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:14.389
The actual wires inside that carry the bits
of code are merely the width of a human hair

00:04:14.389 --> 00:04:17.970
while the cable is much thicker, consisting
of layers of protection.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.650
Still, some hazards are unavoidable.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:25.270
In 2016, a fishing trawler is believed to
have severed a cable in Africa, taking the

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:30.330
whole of the west African country of Mauritania
offline for two days and impacting nine other

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.330
nations.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.970
And in 2013, authorities in Egypt accused
three scuba divers of trying to cut a cable

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.099
in the Mediterranean sea.

00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:40.009
Repairing a line can take weeks.

00:04:40.009 --> 00:04:44.400
A specially equipped ship is dispatched to
the region, pulls up the cable in the right

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.569
spot, makes the repair, before bringing it
back down.

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:52.520
When possible, multiple lines covering the
same route are laid to prevent disruptions.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.569
Besides broken cables, there are also security
concerns.

00:04:55.569 --> 00:05:00.160
In 2020, Washington blocked the portion of
Facebook and Google’s planned cable linking

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.090
the U.S. to Hong Kong over fears of spying because
one of the other backers was majority-owned

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.780
by a Chinese telecom company.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.410
Similarly, in 2018, Australia prevented the
Chinese company Huawei from funding a new

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:16.391
cable to Papua New Guinea and the Solomon
Islands over fears China could use the

00:05:16.391 --> 00:05:20.060
technology to spy on the West which Huawei
has denied.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.009
Even if countries thwart efforts to build
cables, the need for greater data capacity

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:25.169
still exists.

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:27.560
And satellites can’t handle the demand.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.430
SpaceX may be creating a high-speed satellite
internet service, however, Starlink

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:36.229
doesn’t have enough bandwidth to operate
in dense urban areas and will mainly serve

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:37.470
rural regions.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:42.460
So, undersea cables are still the most reliable
way to transmit information to the greatest

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:44.940
number of people over large distances.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.440
There’s another way to stay connected.

